Water rendering and composition performance
==========================================

Scope and contracts
  Remove recurring executable preparation and unnecessary water shader/background work.
  Preserve the existing water-volume model, HDR transport, refracted scattering direction,
  mandatory bilateral receiver filtering, and precompiled UV/x2/x4/x8 quality options.
  Preserve full/half background resolution, opaque receiver validity, OIT ordering, and the
  verified shallow-water banding/sparkle fixes. Performance changes must not restore confidence
  fading, unsupported receiver hits, foreground leakage, or disappearing distortion.
  Reuse shader registry, resource lifetime, pipeline descriptions, StateCache, and profiling owners.
  Do not introduce a new shader-cache or profiling framework for this work.
  Full-scene HDR and replacement of the base-game sky are separate work, not prerequisites.

References
  - docs/PBR.WaterRefraction.md: receiver, traversal, quality and composition contracts.
  - docs/PBR.WaterMedium.md: signed boundary accumulation and physical transport.
  - docs/PBR.Liquids.md: liquid/OIT integration.
  - artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/findings.md: original capture evidence and measurement limitations.
  - docs/Rendering.AuthoritativePipelineState.todo: broader state ownership migration.

Evidence and completed work
  ☑ Retain separate volume and surface LiquidShaderProgram owners.
    - pbr_water_volume retains mode 3; pbr_liquid retains surface mode 0.
    - Removed per-frame mode changes from WaterVolumeRenderer. Existing registry reload and
      disposal own both executables and their independent inputs.
    - Isolated build and 20/20 focused GPU tests passed, including stable IDs during alternating
      use, surface-only quality changes, reload and disposal. See owner-fix-tests.log/.trx in
      artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis. In-game improvement has not yet been measured.
  Original capture: frame 1367 at 3440x1377, before the owner fix. It records two liquid
  program creations/links and approximately 10.45 ms/14.51 ms preparation intervals.
  Those are captured CPU intervals, not GPU pass times or uncaptured gameplay costs.
  Volume/surface fragment counts were approximately 1.95M/1.85M; extreme water overdraw was
  not demonstrated in that view. Generic replay GPU timings were unreliable. The captured
  surface binary matched a Debug artifact whose receipt specifies -O0.

Measurement policy
  Use the same scene, camera, viewport, settings, loaded build and shader optimization policy
  for before/after comparisons. Record CPU preparation separately from GPU draws and frame time.
  Reuse existing timer scopes, adding narrow scopes only where the affected work is unmeasured.
  Do not infer production GPU cost from headless test duration, SPIR-V size, or texture-fetch bounds.
  User-run captures provide live acceptance; do not launch or control the game automatically.
  Missing live measurements must remain explicitly unmeasured, not be replaced by estimates.
  Keep checks proportional to each change; do not require an exhaustive visual matrix for every
  independent optimization. Final targeted comparisons may reuse earlier applicable evidence.

Phase 1 — Remove composite executable replacement
  ☑ Retain independently prepared pre-overlay and ordinary composite owners.
    - Audit actual capture/ordinary callers: capture forces LumOn off; ordinary composition can
      enable it. Both currently mutate one pbr_composite executable through structural options.
    - Keep the capture environment fallback and ordinary LumOn inputs unchanged. Configure
      each owner at its appropriate settings/reload boundary, without per-frame option toggling.
    - Update PrepareFrame consistently, but do not activate deferred full-scene HDR coordination.
    - Preserve frame inputs, binding contracts, registry lifecycle and shared offline binaries.
  ☑ Verify LumOn on/off, refraction enabled/disabled, and alternating capture/ordinary use.
    - Check retained program IDs and absence of repeated asset reads/preparation after warmup.
    - Verify quality/settings changes, reload and disposal without stale inputs or resource leaks.
  Completion: both required composite executables remain resident; alternating passes do not
  create/load/link/delete programs or rebuild their interfaces. Record any live timing separately.
  Verified 2026-10-04: isolated Debug build passed; 29/29 focused GPU tests passed, zero skips.
  Production capture/ordinary draws retain program IDs, interfaces and asset-read counts after
  warmup, including full/half backgrounds, independent PBR/AO changes, refraction disable/re-enable,
  changing receiver inputs, reload and disposal. Second review and independent completion audit
  passed. See artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/composite-owner-build.log, composite-owner-test-build.log
  and composite-owner-tests.log/.trx; ownership is documented in PBR.WaterRefraction.md.
  Initial reload failures were corrected at the synthetic fixture's raw-binding boundary;
  strict GL checks remain. Live timing improvement is unmeasured.

Phase 2 — Supply inverse projection once per frame
  ☑ Add inverse projection to the existing liquid frame binding/UBO contract and frame capture.
    - Reuse the owning matrix convention and existing CPU inversion APIs.
    - Share it across ray tracing, UV distortion and fallback; remove fragment matrix inversions.
    - Update production-importing fixtures and generated binding expectations together.
  ☑ Verify reconstructed positions and refraction across camera views and full/half backgrounds.
    - Confirm compiled production variants no longer contain the redundant matrix inversions.
  Completion: existing rendering behavior is preserved and inverse projection is supplied through
  the normal frame input owner, including diagnostic paths that import the shared algorithms.
  Verified 2026-10-04: fresh isolated Debug build passed; 312/312 GPU regressions passed, zero skips.
  GPU UBO readback verifies asymmetric projection changes, reconstruction and singular-update
  rejection; receiver tests cover full/half backgrounds, UV/ray tiers and shallow continuity.
  All 32 liquid fragment variants, shared vertex and both refraction diagnostic binaries contain
  zero MatrixInverse instructions (35 binaries); the original capture contains two.
  Second review and independent completion audit passed. See artifacts/WaterLagAnalysis/
  inverse-projection-build.log, inverse-projection-tests.log/.trx and inverse-projection-spirv.json.
  The frame layout and matrix convention are documented in PBR.WaterRefraction.md.
  Live performance and optimized Release validation were not measured by this Debug run.

Phase 3 — Separate receiver geometry search from radiance reconstruction
  ☐ Audit the receiver validity contract before deferring color samples.
    - Color alpha currently carries receiver validity; preserve that information during search.
    - Reuse existing depth/source metadata where valid. If a representation change is needed,
      document its producer/consumer contract and costs before implementing it.
  ☐ Avoid loading/interpolating radiance for intermediate receiver probes where validity permits.
    - Retain required depth taps, bilateral layer rejection, source positions, precision bounds,
      slope/plane validation and supported-triangle tests.
    - Reconstruct accepted receiver radiance with the same spatial weights and supported coverage.
    - Preserve UV fallback and full/half source-coordinate semantics.
  ☐ Verify invalid color/depth coverage, foreground occluders, shallow shores, steep walls and edges.
    - Compare actual sample work and rendering against the prior implementation using focused cases.
  Completion: intermediate radiance work is reduced without weakening receiver validation or
  changing accepted shading. Record bounds separately from measured GPU savings.

Phase 4 — Reuse the trace seed in UV fallback
  ☐ Pass the already evaluated interface seed into fallback through the shared receiver contract.
    - Preserve valid and invalid seed outcomes; do not reuse the last marched receiver as the seed.
    - Avoid copying an unnecessarily large payload or extending its lifetime without checking cost.
    - Keep UV-only quality independent and retain total-internal-reflection/early-out behavior.
    - Retain x2/x4/x8 traversal ceilings and separately account for fallback sampling.
  ☐ Verify exhausted traces, invalid initial support, shallow x2 transitions and screen edges.
    - Demonstrate removal of duplicate seed sampling without changing fallback radiance/geometry.
  Completion: fallback reuses equivalent initial support and preserves the existing distortion fixes.
  Dependency: settle Phase 3 receiver payload changes before finalizing seed handoff.

Phase 5 — Skip scattering illumination for zero-scattering media
  ☐ Add a bounded zero-scattering path to volume-boundary shading.
    - Skip shadow/phase/source-light evaluation when all effective scattering coefficients are zero.
    - Preserve absorption, signed optical depth, submerged length and boundary-count accumulation.
    - Preserve nonzero scattering and mixed-material boundary behavior; zero density must stay valid.
    - Audit analogous surface calculations, but do not skip lighting shared with reflections.
  ☐ Verify clear water, authored scattering, mixed boundaries and underwater camera transport.
    - Compare GPU cost for coherent clear-water regions and mixed media; branch overhead may differ.
  Completion: zero-scattering output is unchanged and unnecessary source-light work is avoided;
  do not claim a measured speedup without an applicable comparison.

Phase 6 — Capture refraction backgrounds only when needed
  ☐ Identify an existing authoritative water-demand signal available before the hand overlay.
    - Account for underwater views, partially visible water, frustum/screen transitions, resize,
      first-/third-person rendering, and settings changes. Avoid synchronous GPU readback.
    - Prefer conservative extra capture over missing a required current-frame background.
  ☐ Gate pre-overlay lighting/composition using that signal when it proves capture unnecessary.
    - Invalidate skipped publication so stale backgrounds cannot be consumed.
    - Retain resources across ordinary visibility changes; retire through existing lifecycle owners.
    - Preserve the hand-occlusion exclusion and environment fallback behavior.
    - If no safe signal exists, record the missing ownership contract and measured benefit before
      expanding scope; do not add an unreliable camera-block-only visibility heuristic.
  ☐ Verify dry views, entering/exiting water views, underwater views and first-person overlays.
  Completion: unnecessary captures are skipped without stale or absent required receivers.

Phase 7 — Eliminate discarded fallback shading
  ☐ Skip ordinary fallback shading when a valid refracted receiver fully replaces its contribution.
    - Current successful ray/UV receivers have confidence 1. Preserve the fallback for invalid
      receivers and any supported partial-confidence path; do not force confidence to one.
    - Avoid unused fallback scattering-source, transport and atmospheric calculations, including
      fallback-only work inside the point-light loop. Retain shared reflection lighting.
    - Preserve non-water liquids, total internal reflection, alpha, glow and OIT outputs.
  ☐ Verify valid ray/UV receivers, rejected receivers, refraction disabled and underwater views.
    - Inspect optimized variants and compare work on coherent and mixed receiver-validity regions;
      branch divergence may affect savings.
  Completion: fully replaced fallback contributions are not shaded, with unchanged composition.
  Dependency: integrate with Phase 5 source-light gating rather than adding competing branches.

Phase 8 — Reuse atmospheric transport for the shared camera segment
  ☐ Evaluate surface-to-camera atmospheric attenuation and scattering once when both contributions
    require the same segment, parameters and sky visibility.
    - Coordinate with Phase 7: do not retain a discarded contribution merely to share its samples.
    - Either reuse evaluated transport or apply it after mathematically equivalent premultiplied
      composition. Preserve coverage weighting so additive atmospheric scattering is not doubled.
    - Keep the underwater refracted air segment separate; it is not the same camera segment.
    - Preserve fog ordering and the final display adaptation boundary.
  ☐ Verify full and partial coverage, receiver/fallback paths, sky visibility and underwater exits.
    - Confirm redundant aerial texture samples and coordinate calculations are removed where
      applicable; check optimized output before attributing additional savings.
  Completion: each identical required atmospheric segment is evaluated once without changing
  radiance or alpha. If Phase 7 already removes a duplicate path, record that coverage explicitly.

Phase 9 — Remove unused pre-overlay composite output
  ☐ Specialize the retained capture owner to produce only the background outputs its consumers need.
    - Trace reads of the shared composite color target before removing its capture-time write.
    - Preserve refraction radiance/depth, validity metadata, environment fallback and hand exclusion.
    - Remove calculations used exclusively by the unconsumed ordinary display-color output.
    - Update output contracts, framebuffer routing and pipeline expectations together; preserve
      ordinary composition and prevent attachment feedback or stale-resource consumption.
    - Reuse existing shader-option and resource owners; do not reintroduce executable churn.
  ☐ Verify capture publication, subsequent ordinary composition, resize, reload and failure paths.
    - Compare output values and demonstrate the removed target write/unused shader work.
  Completion: required captures avoid unused display-color production while their background
  outputs remain equivalent. This complements Phase 6, which skips entire unneeded captures.
  Dependency: use the separate retained composite owners established in Phase 1.

Phase 10 — Share homogeneous-medium integration calculations
  ☐ Evaluate extinction, optical depth and transmission once for each identical medium/path pair.
    - Let transmission and scattering integration consume the shared evaluation where both are
      needed. Preserve the optically thin series expansion and zero-extinction limit.
    - Preserve RGB coefficients, nonnegative inputs and physical path-length conventions.
    - Do not calculate scattering-only results on the zero-scattering path from Phase 5.
  ☐ Verify thin/thick paths, zero extinction/scattering, colored media and underwater transport.
    - Inspect optimized shaders first: compiler common-subexpression elimination may already
      remove some duplicate exponentials. Record actual remaining work and measured benefit.
  Completion: medium integration retains numerical stability and avoids demonstrated duplicate
  calculations; do not claim savings for expressions already eliminated by the compiler.

Phase 11 — Avoid unchanged shader-setting allocations
  ☐ Audit steady-state ConfigureOptions callers and measure their allocation/preparation work.
    - ShaderSettingsEditor copies values and creates settings/load-plan objects even when the
      selected values are unchanged. Prioritize render-loop callers with demonstrated repetition.
    - Apply stable selections at existing configuration boundaries or introduce a narrowly scoped
      unchanged-value fast path in the owning API; preserve atomic edits and mutation guards.
    - Preserve aliases, default restoration, inactive selections, reload invalidation and failed
      preparation retry behavior. Do not bypass pending changes using only installed settings.
  ☐ Verify repeated unchanged selections avoid unnecessary work while real edits still prepare
    the correct variants, including edits after failed preparation and asset reload.
  Completion: targeted unchanged settings no longer allocate/rebuild plans unnecessarily, with
  allocation evidence kept separate from executable-lifetime improvements in Phase 1.

Phase 12 — Prepare only required liquid frame inputs
  ☐ Audit active vertex and fragment inputs for volume and surface owners before narrowing capture.
    - The shared capture routine copies colormap rectangles, point-light arrays and fog spheres.
      Omit only arrays proven unused by both stages of the selected volume contract.
    - Preserve wave snapshots, transforms, material/atlas inputs and all required lighting data.
    - Reuse the retained frame-input owner; avoid a new frame-data framework or unnecessary UBO
      layout split. Account for whole-block upload costs as well as CPU array-copy savings.
  ☐ Verify volume/surface alternation, changing light/fog counts, relevant shader options and reload.
    - Ensure skipped fields cannot leave stale data visible to a consumer that requires them.
    - Measure CPU preparation/allocation changes rather than infer them from shorter source code.
  Completion: volume preparation omits proven unused array work while all active inputs remain
  current and independent of surface submission. Preserve the inverse-projection input from Phase 2.

Phase 13 — Stop redundant endpoint probes
  ☐ Detect traversal paths whose next probe cannot advance after clamping to the visible extent.
    - Evaluate the endpoint before terminating; retain any proposal that can move to a different
      supported position. Do not confuse revisiting a texture footprint with identical ray state.
    - Route exhausted progress through the existing validated UV fallback, preserving the seed
      handoff from Phase 4, total internal reflection and x2/x4/x8 maximum probe budgets.
    - Do not accept an unsupported receiver or introduce a tolerance that discards useful movement.
  ☐ Verify invalid endpoint support, failed patch intersections, short extents and screen edges.
    - Compare receiver/fallback results and instrument probe counts in focused fixtures to show
      that repeated identical endpoint evaluations are removed without losing valid hits.
  Completion: unchanged endpoint state does not consume the remaining traversal budget repeatedly;
  useful probes and the existing distortion behavior are preserved.

Phase 14 — Simplify isotropic scattering evaluation
  ☐ Use the constant 1/(4*pi) phase value when effective anisotropy is exactly zero.
    - This applies to nonzero isotropic scattering, independently of Phase 5's zero-scattering path.
    - Omit directional calculations only when they serve that phase evaluation exclusively;
      preserve reflection, refraction and other consumers of the same directions.
    - Preserve positive/negative anisotropy and existing clamping; do not approximate small
      nonzero anisotropy as zero without a separately justified accuracy change.
  ☐ Verify isotropic and anisotropic media under sun and point lighting, above and below water.
    - Inspect optimized variants and measure coherent/mixed-material cases; a dynamic branch
      must not be assumed faster than compiler simplification or the original expression.
  Completion: isotropic phase evaluation retains its exact physical value while demonstrated
  redundant phase arithmetic is removed, without undermining earlier shading-work reductions.

Phase 15 — Reject reduction candidates before color fetches
  ☐ Reorder half-resolution background reduction to reject unusable depth candidates first.
    - Reject invalid/out-of-range depth and candidates that cannot beat the current selected depth
      before fetching their color. Preserve bounds handling for odd source dimensions.
    - Fetch and validate color for surviving candidates before updating the selected receiver.
    - Preserve color-alpha validity, finite-value checks, tie ordering, original source UV metadata
      and the farthest-valid-receiver policy; invalid color must not displace a valid selection.
  ☐ Verify all-invalid blocks, mixed valid/invalid colors, equal depths, silhouettes and odd sizes.
    - Compare reduced outputs with the prior algorithm and confirm reduced color sampling for
      rejected candidates. Measure actual GPU effects separately from source-level fetch counts.
  Completion: reduction produces equivalent receiver pairs with unnecessary color reads avoided.
  Dependency: reconcile any validity representation changes made in Phase 3 before finalizing checks.

Phase 16 — Reuse unchanged uniform-buffer uploads safely
  ☐ Measure repeated whole-block uploads during activation/restoration through CpuUniformBuffer.
    - Reuse the existing ring allocator and binding owners; distinguish this upload work from
      Phase 12's CPU input preparation and Phase 11's shader-setting allocations.
  ☐ Retain and rebind an uploaded range only while both contents and allocation remain valid.
    - Track content revision and the allocator's actual lifetime/generation contract. IsDirty alone
      cannot establish validity after ring reuse, reset, disposal or a different active ring.
    - Preserve immutable earlier draw snapshots when contents change; allocate a fresh range
      rather than overwrite storage still referenced by submitted draws.
    - Rebind the correct slot/range even when upload is skipped. Preserve mutation guards,
      failed-publication behavior, program reload, alignment and block-size validation.
    - Audit existing writable-span/MarkDirty paths so every supported mutation invalidates reuse.
  ☐ Verify unchanged reactivation, changed data, alternating owners, shared blocks, ring rollover,
    allocator replacement and disposal. Include actual GPU-observed contents and upload counts.
    - Check affected non-water consumers because this is a shared rendering-infrastructure change.
  Completion: identical data reuses valid uploaded storage without stale bindings or premature
  allocation reuse; record allocation/copy savings separately from GPU rendering performance.

Phase 17 — Review remaining bandwidth and validate the combined result
  ☐ Obtain a matched optimized-build comparison after executable-lifetime fixes.
    - Separate preparation, water-depth, volume-boundary, surface/OIT, pre-overlay capture and
      ordinary composition costs using existing profiling owners where possible.
    - Compare affected quality/resolution selections and at least a water-heavy and dry view;
      retain build/settings details alongside results. No fixed FPS target is assumed.
  ☐ Investigate remaining volume-target and composition bandwidth only if measurements justify it.
    - Two full-resolution RGBA32F volume targets remain a known cost; storage size is not bandwidth.
    - Evaluate coverage restriction or compatible pass/resource reuse before changing precision.
    - Do not blindly switch signed accumulation to RGBA16F, remove required backfaces, or enable
      depth rejection that loses valid entry/exit boundaries. Any such change needs a separate
      documented numerical/visibility justification and focused regression coverage.
    - Keep measured findings with no justified implementation explicitly documented as such.
  ☐ Review focused regressions, linked documentation and user-run visual/performance feedback.
    - Preserve shallow-wall continuity, x2 stability, receiver rejection and underwater transitions.
    - Record measured improvements, remaining bottlenecks and any unmeasured acceptance items.
  Completion: implemented optimizations have applicable correctness evidence and an honest
  performance record; speculative bandwidth redesign is not an automatic completion requirement.
